  "phase3_gemini_self_healing": {
    "forensics": "The primary loss drivers are mid-day volume decay leading to EOD square-off fee drag (Trades 2, 6, 7, 12) and exhaustion buying from an overly extreme 3.0x sweep volume filter that triggers entries at the very peak of intraday liquidity spikes before immediate mean-reversion (Trades 1, 3, 4, 5). Furthermore, the wide 0.68% stop-loss paired with a late 1.15R breakeven trigger leaves positions vulnerable to full-sized whipsaws when initial momentum stalls, resulting in transaction fees (\u20b955.26) exceeding net losses.",
    "learning": "In NSE large-cap cash equities, requiring a 3.0x volume spike on 5-minute bars selects for climax exhaustion rather than institutional accumulation; moderating the volume threshold while tightening stops and accelerating breakeven triggers is essential to survive mid-day volatility compression and overcome fixed brokerage/STT drag on small capital.",
    "parameter_patch": {
      "min_target_pct": 1.15,
      "min_sweep_vol_multiplier": 2.1,
      "breakeven_trigger_r": 0.85,
      "min_sl_pct": 0.5
    },
    "plan_update_summary": "### Cycle #51 Post-Mortem & Parameter Mutation\nForensic analysis of Cycle #51 revealed severe fee drag (\u20b955.26 fees vs \u20b9-32.05 net P&L) caused by EOD square-offs on stagnant mid-day entries and climax reversal whipsaws in high-beta names like TATASTEEL and ITC. The `min_sweep_vol_multiplier` at 3.0x acted as an exhaustion filter, entering trades at local tops just prior to liquidity mean-reversion. For Cycle #52, we are relaxing `min_sweep_vol_multiplier` to 2.1x to capture earlier institutional ignition, tightening `min_sl_pct` from 0.68% to 0.50% to cap downside per trade, lowering `breakeven_trigger_r` to 0.85R to aggressively neutralize fee drag on stalling mid-day trades, and expanding `min_target_pct` to 1.15% to improve reward-to-risk asymmetry."
  },
